diagnosis_text:
The network path between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and the partner host pc2 (200.1.1.2) is healthy; the slowdown is not due to link congestion. Evidence:
- Latency and loss: End-to-end pings are 0.5–1.6 ms average with 0% loss in both directions. No queueing delay typical of congestion.
- Throughput: iperf from pc1→pc2 sustained ~150 Mbps; while there were some TCP retransmissions, all router interfaces show fq_codel with no backlog or drops, which rules out persistent congestion on the interconnect.
- Queues/interfaces: tc qdisc on router1/2 (eth0/eth1) shows zero drops/overlimits and empty backlogs. Links are up/full-duplex.
- Application reachability: From pc1, HTTP to 200.1.1.2 returns immediately (<1 ms), and pc2’s netstat shows no TCP services listening on any address except the local stub resolver (127.0.0.11). This indicates the target application isn’t running or is blocked on pc2, not that the network is slow.

Localization: Not a routing or interface issue on router1/router2; the problem is at the endpoint pc2 (200.1.1.2) where the expected service is absent/unreachable.

Root cause: The partner host (pc2) is not running the requested application service (e.g., HTTP) or is firewalling it, leading users to perceive “slow” or non-responsive behavior.

Corrective fix: Engage the partner/application team to start the intended service on pc2 and/or open the required port(s). No network “unclogging” or link upgrade is needed; the transport path is performing normally. === END ===